  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 233 AM MDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nWe are in between systems today, leading to mainly dry conditions \nand slightly warmer temperatures for this afternoon and Friday \nafternoon, too. Highs today reach the mid to upper 50s for most of \nus in the lower elevations with more cities reaching the upper 50s \nto almost 60 on Friday afternoon. Winds will be fairly light today \nwith gusts around 15 to 20 mph. \n\nThere is still an area of low pressure sitting off the coast of the \nPacific Northwest and troughing just off the west coast today. This \nlow and trough is gathering Pacific moisture will eject a weak \nshortwave through Idaho on Friday. Wind gusts begin to increase on \nFriday, closer to 20-30 mph for many. This will also bring some \nisolated to scattered showers back to the Central Mountains as early \nas Friday morning with coverage increasing a bit on Friday afternoon \nthrough early Saturday. This first \"quick\" round of precipitation \nwill bring about 0.10 to 0.20 inches of precipitation the the area \nwhich will also mean to expect a few inches of snow up on top of the \nmountains, too. Most of this will be above pass level, but there is \nabout a half inch to an inch of snow in the current forecast for \nGalena Summit from Friday morning to Saturday morning. \n\nIt's late Saturday into Sunday that the low itself begins to move \nand makes it way inland and deep troughing which will really pick up \nmore Pacific moisture and transport it to us. This more organized \nround of precipitation will begin in the Central Mountains/Wood \nRiver Valley/eastern Magic Valley as early as 6 PM Saturday and then \nspread eastward throughout the night. The NBM is now showing about \nan 80 to 100 percent chance of the entire CWA receiving at least a \nquarter of an inch of precipitation from 5 AM Saturday to 5 AM \nMonday. It seems likely that many will see 0.25 to 0.50 inches in \nthe eastern Magic Valley, 0.50 to 0.75 inches in the Snake River \nPlain and lower elevations of the Eastern Highlands and Central \nMountains, while the higher elevations will be pushing 0.80 to 1.50 \ninches. Temperatures will still be \"warm\" on Saturday with highs in \nthe 50s, but by Sunday our highs will drop into the mid and upper \n40s. Snow impacts seem likely at Galena Summit Saturday night into \nSunday morning with the forecast currently showing 4-6\" of snow \nthere. Elsewhere, the bulk of our snow is expected to remain above \nmost of the rest of our mountain passes, but that could change \neasily with temperature changes of just a few degrees.\n\nA few showers linger on Monday morning, but we will be drying out \nfrom west to east by that point and high pressure builds in over the \narea through mid week.",
